 "drivingQuestion": "Why is gentile ḥiltit forbidden?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "reason given and anticipated difficulty answered",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "avodah_zarah.39a.12",
   "daf": "39a",
   "sentenceId": 11,
   "he": "וְהַקּוֹרֶט שֶׁל חִילְתִּית. מַאי טַעְמָא? מִשּׁוּם דְּמַפְסְקִי לֵיהּ בְּסַכִּינָא. אַף עַל גַּב דְּאָמַר מָר: נוֹתֵן טַעַם לִפְגָם מוּתָּר, אַגַּב חוּרְפֵּיהּ דְּחִילְתִּיתָא מְחַלְּיָא לֵיהּ שַׁמְנוּנִיתָא, וְהָוֵה לֵיהּ כְּנוֹתֵן טַעַם לְשֶׁבַח, וְאָסוּר.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And a sliver of ḥiltit» may not be consumed, although one may derive benefit from it. The Gemara asks: «What is the reason» that it is prohibited? It is «because they slice it with a knife» on which there is presumably non-kosher residue. And «even though the Master said» that a prohibited substance that «imparts flavor to» the «detriment» of the mixture is «permitted,» that principle does not apply in this case because «as a result of the sharpness of the ḥiltit,» the act of slicing it with a knife «sweetens,» i.e., enhances, the taste of «the» non-kosher «residue. And» there-fore «it is like» a prohibited substance that «imparts flavor to» the «enhancement» of the mixture, «and» it is «prohibited.»"
  }
 ],
